[{"order":1,"type":"heading","text":"Over-the-Air (OTA) Updates: CodePush, Expo Updates, and Rollout Strategies in React Native"},{"order":2,"type":"paragraph","text":"Over-the-Air (OTA) updates allow React Native developers to push JavaScript, asset, and styling changes directly to users' devices without going through the lengthy Apple App Store or Google Play Store review processes. This tutorial covers the two dominant ecosystems (CodePush and Expo Updates) and outlines enterprise rollout strategies."},{"order":3,"type":"heading","text":"1. Understanding React Native OTA Mechanics"},{"order":4,"type":"paragraph","text":"React Native apps consist of a native binary (Objective-C/Swift for iOS, Java/Kotlin for Android) and a JavaScript bundle. OTA updates target only the JavaScript bundle and static assets. If you modify native modules, add new native dependencies via linking, or change app permissions, you MUST submit a new binary to the app stores. OTA is strictly for JS-level changes."},{"order":5,"type":"heading","text":"2. Implementing Expo Updates"},{"order":6,"type":"paragraph","text":"If you are building with Expo (managed or bare workflow), Expo Updates is the native-integrated solution for delivering OTA updates."},{"order":7,"type":"subheading","text":"Installation and Configuration"},{"order":8,"type":"paragraph","text":"First, install the expo-updates package in your project."},{"order":9,"type":"code","text":"npx expo install expo-updates"},{"order":10,"type":"paragraph","text":"Next, configure your app.json to include the updates runtime version and URL endpoint."},{"order":11,"type":"code","text":"{
  "expo": {
    "name": "My App",
    "slug": "my-app",
    "runtimeVersion": {
      "policy": "sdkVersion"
    },
    "updates": {
      "url": "https://u.expo.dev/YOUR-PROJECT-ID"
    }
  }
}"},{"order":12,"type":"subheading","text":"Checking for Updates Programmatically"},{"order":13,"type":"paragraph","text":"You can check and apply updates directly inside your React Native entry file."},{"order":14,"type":"code","text":"import * as Updates from 'expo-updates';
import React, { useEffect } from 'react';
import { Text, View } from 'react-native';

export default function App() {
  useEffect(() => {
    async function checkForUpdates() {
      try {
        const update = await Updates.checkForUpdateAsync();
        if (update.isAvailable) {
          await Updates.fetchUpdateAsync();
          await Updates.reloadAsync();
        }
      } catch (error) {
        console.error('Error fetching Expo update:', error);
      }
    }
    checkForUpdates();
  }, []);

  return (
    <View>
      <Text>Welcome to my updated app!</Text>
    </View>
  );
}"},{"order":15,"type":"heading","text":"3. Implementing Microsoft CodePush"},{"order":16,"type":"paragraph","text":"For bare React Native CLI projects, Microsoft CodePush (via App Center) is the traditional industry standard for managing JS bundles."},{"order":17,"type":"subheading","text":"Setup and Integration"},{"order":18,"type":"paragraph","text":"Install the react-native-code-push library."},{"order":19,"type":"code","text":"npm install --save react-native-code-push
cd ios && pod install"},{"order":20,"type":"paragraph","text":"Wrap your root component using the codePush higher-order component."},{"order":21,"type":"code","text":"import React from 'react';
import { Text, View } from 'react-native';
import codePush from 'react-native-code-push';

function App() {
  return (
    <View>
      <Text>Hello CodePush!</Text>
    </View>
  );
}

let codePushOptions = { checkFrequency: codePush.CheckFrequency.ON_APP_START };

export default codePush(codePushOptions)(App);"},{"order":22,"type":"heading","text":"4. Enterprise Rollout Strategies"},{"order":23,"type":"paragraph","text":"Deploying OTA updates to 100% of your user base instantly is a high-risk anti-pattern. If a critical bug slips into your JS bundle, you risk breaking your entire user population. Implement these rollout strategies:"},{"order":24,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Staged Rollouts: Release updates to a small percentage of users (e.g., 5%) first. Monitor crash reporting tools like Sentry or Bugsnag before expanding to 25%, 50%, and 100%."},{"order":25,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Targeted Channels: Maintain distinct environments such as 'Staging', 'QA', and 'Production'. Force QA builds to point to the Staging channel."},{"order":26,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Rollback Mechanisms: Ensure your OTA provider supports instant rollbacks. If a bad bundle is detected, you must be able to revert to the previous stable release immediately."},{"order":27,"type":"heading","text":"5. Compliance and Store Guidelines"},{"order":28,"type":"paragraph","text":"Both Apple (App Store Review Guidelines 2.5.2) and Google Play permit OTA updates under the strict condition that the updates do not fundamentally change the primary purpose of the app, introduce executable code that downloads unrelated features, or bypass the core review mechanisms for critical safety and security parameters."}]
